WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — three-body (90s) Narrator Bill

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:06.778
First, most big computer failures happen at the handoffs between teams or parts, not inside them.

2
00:00:07.278 --> 00:00:13.298
Second, the cause is usually a guess nobody wrote down, like which units a number uses.

3
00:00:13.798 --> 00:00:18.328
Third, when every handoff guess is written down and machine-checked,

4
00:00:18.328 --> 00:00:20.394
a fix in one place stays fixed.

5
00:00:20.894 --> 00:00:26.206
The Ariane 5 rocket broke apart forty seconds into its maiden flight because inertial

6
00:00:26.206 --> 00:00:32.267
guidance code reused from slower Ariane 4 overflowed a sixteen-bit velocity number. Earlier, Mars

7
00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:39.329
Climate Orbiter burned up over pound-versus-newton thruster units—both unwritten guesses crossing a team handoff.

8
00:00:39.829 --> 00:00:46.459
Computing couples three distinct roles: theorists who state mathematical models, makers who build chips

9
00:00:46.459 --> 00:00:52.702
and compilers, and users who run real workloads. Just as Lagrange points stabilize three orbiting

10
00:00:52.702 --> 00:01:00.104
bodies by locking their relative frame, explicit machine-checked contracts prevent boundary drift across all three.

11
00:01:00.604 --> 00:01:05.841
Independent Csmith fuzzing uncovered hundreds of wrong-code bugs in every C compiler

12
00:01:05.841 --> 00:01:12.263
tested except CompCert's machine-checked middle passes, while the verified seL4 operating-system kernel

13
00:01:12.263 --> 00:01:17.687
and formal cloud blueprints eliminated whole bug classes inside their stated contracts.

14
00:01:18.187 --> 00:01:23.509
Pursuing artificial general intelligence across every layer of the software stack leaves

15
00:01:23.509 --> 00:01:29.496
zero room for hallway folklore—turning chaotic whack-a-mole debugging into steady, lasting repairs.
